  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Empress Elisabeth is a 56-year-old woman with anorexia nervosa, restricting type, in outpatient treatment, and recurrent depression, who comes for a preventive visit between her eating disorder reviews. She has followed most of the meal plan, with her dietitian&apos;s help by video while she was away for ten days. She walks for about an hour a day and has not used the gymnastics apparatus. She started sertraline 25 mg three weeks ago and increased to 50 mg a week ago, with mild nausea for two days. She still has passive thoughts of being better off dead on some days, less often, with no intent or plan; she keeps her safety plan with her. She stopped having periods in her late forties. She asked that this visit not be used to weigh her in front of anyone. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Periodic adult preventive visit, age 56</td></tr><tr><td>Eating disorder</td><td>Following most of the meal plan; walks about an hour a day</td></tr><tr><td>Sertraline</td><td>50 mg for a week; mild nausea for two days</td></tr><tr><td>Mood</td><td>PHQ-9 13; passive thoughts less often; no intent or plan</td></tr><tr><td>Menopause</td><td>Last period in her late forties</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Completed by the patient on paper in a private room and reviewed with her face to face by Dr. Marchetti-Osei. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Fair; less dizzy than before</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Low mood and grief; supportive companion; dislikes public attention</td></tr><tr><td>Tobacco</td><td>Never</td></tr><tr><td>Alcohol</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Physical activity</td><td>Walks about an hour a day, limited by the treatment plan</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Three meals and two snacks on the dietitian&apos;s plan</td></tr><tr><td>Sleep</td><td>Six hours, broken</td></tr><tr><td>Sexual health</td><td>No concerns raised</td></tr><tr><td>Safety</td><td>Feels faint less often; no falls; walks alone on country roads</td></tr><tr><td>Road safety</td><td>Wears a seat belt; does not drive</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent</td></tr><tr><td>Hearing and vision</td><td>Normal by her report</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

