  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Anorexia nervosa, restricting type, moderate (DSM-5-TR), with compulsive exercise and frequent self-weighing, beginning in early adulthood; complicated at diagnosis by sinus bradycardia, orthostatic dizziness, ankle edema, mild anemia and leukopenia, low glucose, raised ALT and low phosphate after refeeding began</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>F50.011</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis made at the consultation, linked to her documented restrictive eating and exercise and to the retrospective diagnosis of anorexia nervosa (R).</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Major depressive disorder, recurrent, moderate, with persistent grief for her son, who died five years ago; passive thoughts of death without intent or plan at intake</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>F33.1</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis linked to her documented mourning and ever-deepening depressions; not a claim that she was so diagnosed.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-5">
    <td>Low bone mass (osteopenia) of the lumbar spine and hip after decades of low weight: lumbar spine T-score -2.1, femoral neck T-score -1.8; FRAX 10-year probabilities 6.8% for a major osteoporotic fracture and 1.2% for hip fracture; no fractures</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>M85.80</td><td>S — Synthetic modern finding on DXA; not a historical claim.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

